> Regarding V1 being a variable.
>
> That's exactly what irritates me.
>
> When one looks at the structure of the original data.frame "a.df" it says
> that it is a data.frame of 5 obs with 8 variables (V1 to V3 and X1 to X3
> being some of them besides Test.no and Test.goal). After melting the data
> only the 2 defined id-variables  "Test.no" and "Test.goal" plus "variables"
> (as factors) and "values" are left in the melted frame.

Right, that's what melt does. It you want V1 to stay a separate column
you need to include it in the id.vars argument.

>
> So when I try to cast the data back to an alternative form pulling for
> instance V1 back into the data.frame columns, V1 is no longer recognized as
> a variable.

Right, but this is expected and normal operation of melt. You don't
specify V1 to put it back in a column; instead you specify the
variable identifying which are the V1 rows, and put that in the
columns, as you've already done with

dcast(a.m, formula= Test.no + Test.goal ~ ...)

