Hi:

I think you're looking at this problem the wrong way - reshaping the
data frame won't get what you appear to want, which are matrices of
co-indications of V levels with X levels within test. This reproduces
what I think you're after, but you only supplied the expected output
from test goal A (which appears to be confounded with test number), so
I did a bit of mental extrapolation.

Here's a function that generates a co-indication matrix by row of the
initial data frame, returning the rows where any of X1-X3 is nonzero.
As you can tell by its code, it's *very* specific to your example.

coind <- function(x)
{
    x1 <- x[1:3]
    x2 <- x[4:6]
    m <- cbind(kronecker(rep(1, 3), diag(3)), kronecker(diag(x2), x1))
    select_row <- function(x) any(x[4:6] > 0)
    m[apply(m, 1, select_row), ]
}

# The first call to kronecker() creates an 'rbind'-ed identity matrix
(in this case, 3).
# The second call matches the co-indications of the V's with the X's.
# The last line uses select_row() to cull any rows where columns 4-6
are all zero.

# Applying this to each row of the data frame, we first convert the V/X columns
# into a matrix, then create a list object where each component is a row of the
# matrix. Apply the above function to each list component. The end result
# is a data frame whose columns we rename at the end to match those of a.df,
# sans the first variable.

mat <- as.matrix(a.df[, -c(1, 2)]

library(plyr)
d <- adply(mat, 1, coind)
names(d) <- c("Test.no", paste0("V", 1:3), paste0("X", 1:3))

#------
If you want the test goal in there, too, just do
d["Test.Goal"] <- LETTERS[d$Test.no]

